---
title: Bread Stuffing
course: side
cuisine: american
servings: 10
time_active: 30
time_total: 150
leftovers: great
tags: [holiday]
source: https://www.bigoven.com/recipe/bread-stuffing-101/150746
description: Classic bread stuffing with sweated vegetables and poultry herbs, baked in a dish.
---

Cut @rustic sandwich bread{1 1/2-2%lb} into 1-inch cubes, about 16 cups. Spread on #sheet pans{} and dry at 250°F until dry but not browned, ~{1%hour}, or leave out overnight.

Melt @butter{12%tbsp} in a large #skillet{} over medium-low. Add @yellow onions{2}(chopped), @carrots{2}(chopped), and @celery{4%stalks}(chopped). Cook, stirring often, until soft and golden without browning, ~{10%minutes}.

Scrape the vegetables and butter into a large bowl. Add the bread and @fresh parsley{1%bunch}(chopped).

Stir in @chicken stock{4%cups}(low-sodium), a little at a time, until the stuffing is moist.

Season with @dried rosemary{1 1/2%tsp}(crumbled), @dried sage{1 1/2%tsp}, @dried thyme{1 1/2%tsp}, @dried marjoram{1 1/2%tsp}, @celery salt{1 1/2%tsp}, and @black pepper{1/2%tsp}. Taste and add @salt as needed.

Heat the oven to 350°F. Spread in a buttered #9x13 baking dish{} and drizzle with @chicken stock{3/4%cup}. Bake covered ~{30%minutes}, then uncovered until the top is crisp, ~{15-20%minutes}.

> Bake it in the dish rather than in the bird: the turkey cooks more evenly and the brined drippings won't oversalt it. The bread can be cubed and dried a day or two ahead.
